  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   We will employ a multi-resolution, multi-start optimization approach. The central idea is to build upon the parent solution and then systematically explore higher resolutions in a structured manner. This is motivated by the observation that higher resolutions can provide smoother representations that may yield better overlap minimization.\n\n   We adverthan the current record of C\u22480.3808 and aim to prove it is \u22640.3800, though we keep the primary goal flexible for conformity.\n\n   The optimization approach involves:\n   1. Repair and constraint enforcement\n   2. Projection-based reparameterization\n   3. Scored-based selection and update\n   4. Progressive expansion and repair\n   5. Budget management across points\n\n   The key limitation is that while the initial plan focused on C-specific minimization, we recognize the inherent non-convexity and large dimensionality requires careful tuning.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   Variables:\n   - h: `n_points`-dimensional vector of step function samples\n   - dx = 2.0 / n_points\n   - Integral constraint: \u2211(h[i] * dx) = 1\n   - Lag evaluation: corr_k = covolve(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n   - Objective: minimize C5 = max_k [dx * corr_k]\n\n   The formulation requires projecting the parent solution across dimensions and ensuring strict adherence to [0,1] and sum constraints through constrained optimization with CVXPY using SOCP formulation. Though highly restricted, this ensures feasibility.\n\n   We assume the use of d-dimensional constrained optimization with equality to handle the integral constraint via reparametrization (introducing a baseline and distribution vector).\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Validate parent and prepare initial storage\n   - Over a budget window:\n        For each resolution n in possible_values:\n            Transform parent to n-uncertainty space (if initialized)\n            Use SOCP projection to move towards optimal (ones * baseline sum)\n            Track improvement-proportional samples\n         Use a repair mechanism for bound and sum\n   - Maintain separate budgets for parent inheritance and new projections\n   - For repair, employ projection in the entire feature space to enforce bounds and sum\n   - Helper functions to scale and normalize\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   Primary: Sample repair-first (ensure validity before optimization). Use properties diffusion limit if time allows.\n   Varations: Multi-initial (10 seeds) and dimension-expansion (doubling/halving)\n   Always use projected gradient in the coordinate space for local descent.\n\n5. Numerical robustness:\n   - Memory: 2 * max_dimension (\u2272 180)\n   - Sum consistency checks every optimization step\n   - Repair \u00e0 la projection matrix\n   - Convergence: Track best and fallback\n\n6. Runtim safeguards:\n   Timer per resolution with timeout handling\n   Early termination options\n   Fallback: inherit parent\n   Form the run:",
